has_wiki":true,"has_pages":false,"has_discussions":false,"forks_count":1201,"mirror_url":null,"archived":false,"disabled":false,"open_issues_count":7608,"license":{"key":"other","name":"Other","spdx_id":"NOASSERTION","url":null,"node_id":"MDc6TGljZW5zZTA="},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"topics":["cloud-native","cpp","database","distributed-database","distributed-sql","distributed-sql-database","high-performance","kubernetes","multi-cloud","multi-region","scale-out","sql"],"visibility":"public","forks":1201,"open_issues":7608,"watchers":9958,"default_branch":"master","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"body":"Jira Link: [DB-4741](https://yugabyte.atlassian.net/browse/DB-4741)\r\nIn #11932, we found that `FOR UPDATE` queries would incorrectly allow simultaneous locking of the same row. This was because we were acquiring only `kStrongRead` in-memory locks on the specified rows fully qualified DocDB key. Still, the intents written at the full DocDB key correctly specified `[kStrongRead, kStrongWrite]`, so conflicts wouldn't be detected until at least one of the intents was written.\r\n\r\nIn general, there should never be a mismatch between the in-memory locks acquired during a `WriteQuery` vs. the intents written applying the same. We should add some debug-only logic or a series of tests to ensure this invariant is never violated.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/11995/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/11995/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"completed"}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"id":6381096435,"node_id":"LE_lADOBlCVUc5GvnS0zwAAAAF8V83z","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/events/6381096435","actor":{"login":"tverona1","id":1056